$A=\{2,3,5,7,11\}$

$B=\{1,3,5,7,9\}$

The diagram places common elements in the overlap.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (7,4);
\draw (2.6,2) circle (1.6);
\draw (4.4,2) circle (1.6);
\node at (1.7,3.15) {$A$};
\node at (5.3,3.15) {$B$};
\node at (1.7,2.2) {$2$};
\node at (1.7,1.5) {$11$};
\node at (3.5,2.7) {$3$};
\node at (3.5,2) {$5$};
\node at (3.5,1.3) {$7$};
\node at (5.3,2.2) {$1$};
\node at (5.3,1.5) {$9$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The union includes every element in either set, listed once.

$A\cup B=\{1,2,3,5,7,9,11\}$

The intersection includes only elements in both sets.

$A\cap B=\{3,5,7\}$
